Use \OnxCodeTerm{--kernel-imports} to inspect the source request without a review. With independently supplied evidence, follow the build in the kernel review manual and test saturation, valid intervals and reversed bounds. Changing the provider identity requires new evidence. A plain compiler check reports that the kernel is unadmitted when run on this listing without its reviewed provider. \cref{ch:95} explains the contract data reused for those decisions.

\section{Decide which direction a string may travel}
Consider a function taking a direct \OnxCodeTerm{const char *} parameter
and returning the same C type. Draw one arrow into the provider and one
out of it. On the first arrow put a call-scoped immutable borrow: a
nonnull sequence of bytes followed by NUL, readable for the duration
of the call. The provider may inspect it but may not save its address
for later. On the second arrow put static immutable storage: the result
must still be readable after the caller has made another call. The two
C type names match, yet interchanging the annotations would change the
programs the caller could safely write.

Compare three proposed implementations. One always returns the address
of immutable static text; it can meet the lifetime requirement, subject
to the other gates. A second returns its argument. Using a static test
literal as input can disguise that alias, but the contract offers no
static lifetime for an arbitrary input. A third returns the address of
a scratch buffer overwritten on every call. Saving the first result,
calling again with different text and reading the saved result reveals
why the immutable-result promise fails. Successful prototype checks
would not distinguish these three bodies.

There are two separate numerical limits. Excluding the terminator, the
adapter admits at most two MiB on the input arrow and four MiB on the
output arrow. A newline is a byte within those limits; it is not an end
marker. An empty string has zero data bytes but still requires a valid
terminator. A null pointer is not a second representation of empty.
Null or excessive length gives terminal status 72, rather than a
recoverable empty result. These checks rely on the underlying storage
being readable; the length scan cannot establish pointer provenance.
When reading a probe report, interpret a recorded length accordingly:
it measures that particular call, not all future lifetimes or effects.

Compare the two kinds of failure with the clamp example. A reversed
interval fails the source adapter's ordered-bounds assertion before
the call to C and exits with status 70. Correcting that interval
repairs a value precondition. It would not repair a stale review
subject or turn a borrowed input pointer into a static string result.

